# SE 4220 Final Project — Gallery Application on GCP

## Overview

This project deploys the **Photo Gallery** web application on Google Cloud Platform (GCP) using Terraform for infrastructure-as-code (IaC). The application allows users to register, log in, upload photos, and search their photo gallery. All infrastructure is provisioned automatically through Terraform.

---

## Architecture

```
                        ┌─────────────────────────────────────┐
                        │         GCP Project                  │
                        │   se-4220-final-project              │
                        │                                      │
         Internet       │  ┌──────────────────────────────┐   │
        ─────────►      │  │        gallery-vpc            │   │
         HTTP/HTTPS     │  │        (10.0.0.0/16)          │   │
                        │  │                               │   │
                        │  │  ┌────────────────────────┐  │   │
                        │  │  │   gallery-subnet        │  │   │
                        │  │  │   (10.0.0.0/16)         │  │   │
                        │  │  │                         │  │   │
                        │  │  │  ┌──────────────────┐   │  │   │
                        │  │  │  │  gallery-vm       │   │  │   │
                        │  │  │  │  e2-standard-2    │   │  │   │
                        │  │  │  │  Flask :5001      │   │  │   │
                        │  │  │  └────────┬─────────┘   │  │   │
                        │  │  │           │              │  │   │
                        │  │  │  ┌────────▼─────────┐   │  │   │
                        │  │  │  │  Cloud SQL Proxy  │   │  │   │
                        │  │  │  └────────┬─────────┘   │  │   │
                        │  │  └───────────┼─────────────┘  │   │
                        │  │             │                  │   │
                        │  │  ┌──────────▼──────────────┐  │   │
                        │  │  │  Cloud SQL MySQL 8.0     │  │   │
                        │  │  │  gallery-db              │  │   │
                        │  │  │  (private IP only)       │  │   │
                        │  │  └─────────────────────────┘  │   │
                        │  └──────────────────────────────┘   │
                        │                                      │
                        │  ┌──────────────────────────────┐   │
                        │  │  Cloud Storage Bucket         │   │
                        │  │  gallery-photos-se4220        │   │
                        │  │  (photo uploads)              │   │
                        │  └──────────────────────────────┘   │
                        └─────────────────────────────────────┘
```

### Components

| Resource | Name | Spec |
|---|---|---|
| VPC Network | gallery-vpc | Custom, no auto subnets |
| Subnet | gallery-subnet | 10.0.0.0/16, us-central1 |
| Firewall | allow-http | TCP 80, 443 |
| Firewall | allow-ssh | TCP 22 |
| Firewall | allow-app | TCP 5001 |
| Compute VM | gallery-vm | e2-standard-2, Debian 11 |
| Cloud SQL | gallery-db | MySQL 8.0, db-n1-standard-1 |
| GCS Bucket | gallery-photos-se4220 | Photo storage |
| Service Account | gallery-sa | Least-privilege IAM |

---

## Prerequisites

- [Terraform](https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/downloads) >= 1.0
- [Google Cloud SDK](https://cloud.google.com/sdk/docs/install)
- A GCP project with billing enabled
- The following APIs enabled:
  - Compute Engine API
  - Cloud SQL Admin API
  - Service Networking API
  - Cloud Storage API

---

## Setup Instructions

### 1. Authenticate with GCP

```bash
gcloud auth application-default login
```

### 2. Clone or prepare the project files

Ensure you have all `.tf` files in your project directory:
- `main.tf`
- `variables.tf`
- `outputs.tf`
- `provider.tf`
- `requirements.tf`
- `backend.tf`
- `startup.sh`

### 3. Create the GCS backend bucket

```bash
gsutil mb gs://YOUR-STATE-BUCKET-NAME
```

### 4. Create a `terraform.tfvars` file

```hcl
project_id  = "your-gcp-project-id"
db_password = "your-secure-password"
bucket_name = "your-photos-bucket-name"
```

### 5. Initialize Terraform

```bash
terraform init
```

### 6. Preview the infrastructure plan

```bash
terraform plan
```

### 7. Apply the infrastructure

```bash
terraform apply
```

Type `yes` when prompted. This will provision:
- VPC, subnet, and firewall rules
- Service account with IAM roles
- Cloud SQL MySQL instance (takes ~5 minutes)
- Compute Engine VM

### 8. Deploy the application to the VM

SSH into the VM:
```bash
gcloud compute ssh gallery-vm --zone=us-central1-a
```

Install dependencies and start the Cloud SQL proxy:
```bash
pip3 install flask exifread python-dotenv mysql-connector-python google-cloud-storage gunicorn
sudo mkdir -p /cloudsql
sudo /usr/local/bin/cloud_sql_proxy -instances=YOUR_PROJECT:us-central1:gallery-db -dir=/cloudsql &
```

Initialize the database tables:
```bash
python3 - <<'EOF'
import mysql.connector
conn = mysql.connector.connect(
    user='gallery', password='YOUR_PASSWORD',
    database='gallerydb',
    unix_socket='/cloudsql/YOUR_PROJECT:us-central1:gallery-db'
)
c = conn.cursor()
c.execute('''CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS users (
    userID INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,
    username VARCHAR(50) UNIQUE NOT NULL,
    password VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL
)''')
c.execute('''CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS photos (
    PhotoID INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,
    userID VARCHAR(100),
    CreationTime DATETIME,
    Title VARCHAR(255),
    Description TEXT,
    Tags VARCHAR(255),
    URL TEXT,
    ExifData JSON
)''')
conn.commit()
conn.close()
print("Tables created!")
EOF
```

Run the application:
```bash
python3 app.py
```

### 9. Access the application

The app will be available at:
```
http://VM_PUBLIC_IP:5001
```

The public IP is shown in the Terraform outputs after `terraform apply`.

---

## Terraform Outputs

After a successful `terraform apply`, the following outputs are displayed:

| Output | Description |
|---|---|
| `vm_public_ip` | Public IP address of the Compute Engine VM |
| `app_url` | Full URL to access the Gallery application |
| `cloud_sql_connection_name` | Cloud SQL connection string for the proxy |

---

## File Structure

```
final-project/
├── main.tf               # Main infrastructure resources
├── variables.tf          # Input variable definitions
├── outputs.tf            # Output value definitions
├── provider.tf           # Google Cloud provider config
├── requirements.tf       # Terraform and provider version constraints
├── backend.tf            # GCS remote state backend config
├── startup.sh            # VM startup script
├── terraform.tfvars      # Variable values (do not commit!)
└── photogallery/
    ├── app.py            # Flask application
    ├── requirements.txt  # Python dependencies
    ├── templates/        # HTML templates
    └── static/           # CSS, JS, assets
```

---

## Teardown

To destroy all provisioned infrastructure:

```bash
terraform destroy
```

Type `yes` when prompted. This will delete all GCP resources created by Terraform.

> **Note:** The GCS state bucket and photos bucket must be emptied and deleted manually if no longer needed.

---

## Environment Variables

The application requires a `.env` file in the `photogallery/` directory:

```
FLASK_SECRET_KEY=your-secret-key
GCS_BUCKET=your-photos-bucket-name
DB_USER=gallery
DB_PASSWORD=your-db-password
DB_NAME=gallerydb
```

---

## Health Check

The application exposes a login page at `/login` which can be used to validate the deployment is running correctly. A successful HTTP 200 response from `http://VM_IP:5001/login` confirms the app and database connection are working.